  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The contour crawl', note: 'Pure contour: draw the edge of a crumpled paper at 2 mm/second, eyes on the object not the page — the result is a mess and the EYE changes permanently: you have never actually looked at anything that slowly before.' },
    { math: '2 · Measure relationships, not things', note: 'Nobody draws "a hand" — you draw angles against verticals, widths relative to lengths, alignments (where does the elbow hit the hip?). Comparative measurement converts objects into geometry you can verify.' },
    { math: '3 · Block-in before detail', note: 'Big shapes, correct proportions, tilts and gesture FIRST; details are earned last. The classic beginner inversion (rendering a perfect eye on a wrong head) is process failure, not talent failure.' },
    { math: '4 · Sight-size and the classical atelier', note: 'Subject and drawing at identical size and distance, compared directly — the 19th-century academies\' calibration method, revived in today\'s ateliers. Ruthless, slow, and it works.' },
