<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/intermediate/drawing-from-observation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Observation drawing is <strong>a trainable seeing discipline</strong> — not a gift. The
    exercises are calibrated honesty drills: slow the eye, measure everything, earn the details.
    Every serious drawing curriculum is a variation on this lesson\'s four practices.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="contour, measurement, block-in, sight-size" widget="equationStep" config={obsCfg} />

  <h2>The subjects in sequence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Still life first:</strong> it doesn\'t move, its geometry is knowable, its lighting is yours — the laboratory subject. Then portrait (the psychology problem), then figure (the everything problem — moving, alive, proportioned from memory when it shifts).</li>
    <li><strong>Memory drawing after observation:</strong> the training lesson\'s retrieval effect — draw the object from memory, then check; the gap between remembered and seen is your perceptual blind spot map.</li>
    <li><strong>Plein air for judgement:</strong> light changes every 20 minutes outdoors — the drill forces decisions and commitment (the value lesson\'s structure, under a clock).</li>
    <li><strong>The 10,000 pots:</strong> quantity with feedback beats preciousness — art education\'s version of the training lesson\'s deliberate practice, and the empirical answer to "can I learn to draw?" (yes: it\'s the method, not the muse).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nicolaïdes; the atelier sight-size literature; the sports lesson\'s deliberate-practice principle (it transfers to craft identically).</p>
</TopicPage>
